Without it FLAG_untrusted_code_mitigations defaults to true, so
mksnapshot builds every bytecode handler with a speculation-poison
prologue that uses isel (POWER7+, illegal on G3/G4/G5). Bootstrapping
executes no bytecode in 8.3 (JS natives are long gone), so mksnapshot
completes and bakes the poisoned handlers into the snapshot — and d8
then SIGILLs on the first bytecode of any script. node ships with
v8_untrusted_code_mitigations=0 (common.gypi) for the same reason.
The define only takes effect in flags.cc (FLAG_MODE_DEFINE), which is
compiled into v8_base_without_compiler, a v8_defines target.
---
 CMakeLists.txt | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/CMakeLists.txt b/CMakeLists.txt
index 7c16abc..890d8eb 100644
--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -83,6 +83,13 @@ set(
   $<${is-arm64}:V8_TARGET_ARCH_ARM64>
   $<${is-ppc}:V8_TARGET_ARCH_PPC>
   $<${is-ppc64}:V8_TARGET_ARCH_PPC64>
+  # Spectre-style untrusted-code mitigations default ON unless this is
+  # defined (node and Chrome desktop define it). The poison prologue uses
+  # isel (POWER7+), which SIGILLs on G3/G4/G5 the moment any bytecode
+  # handler runs; mksnapshot survives only because bootstrapping no
+  # longer executes bytecode.
+  $<${is-ppc}:DISABLE_UNTRUSTED_CODE_MITIGATIONS>
+  $<${is-ppc64}:DISABLE_UNTRUSTED_CODE_MITIGATIONS>
 )
 
 set(D ${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/v8)
